WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:02.260
Welcome to Math one oh two.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:06.520
In Section five point one, we explore area between curves.

3
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.440
Let's see how a familiar rectangle gives us the whole idea.

4
00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.770
Start with the area under one curve.

5
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.950
Each rectangle reaches up to the curve, and its width is delta x.

6
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.910
Height times width gives one small area.

7
00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:24.530
Add more, thinner rectangles, and watch the edges fit the curve more closely.

8
00:00:25.043 --> 00:00:26.743
Keep making the rectangles thinner.

9
00:00:27.303 --> 00:00:29.963
In the limit, their sum becomes the exact area.

10
00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:35.983
That's what the integral means here: we accumulate tiny areas, with every height read from the picture.

11
00:00:36.427 --> 00:00:37.927
Now put a second curve underneath.

12
00:00:38.707 --> 00:00:43.427
Our top curve is three minus x; the bottom curve is x squared minus nine.

13
00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:46.467
We'll preview the interval from negative four to three.

14
00:00:47.027 --> 00:00:51.047
Watch the vertical gap as the strip moves: its height changes with both curves.

15
00:00:51.643 --> 00:00:53.863
The bottom curve dips below the x-axis.

16
00:00:54.543 --> 00:00:59.123
So let's measure both pieces from one temporary line, y equals negative ten.

17
00:00:59.703 --> 00:01:01.643
The blue measurement reaches the top curve.

18
00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:04.303
The red measurement reaches the bottom curve.

19
00:01:04.803 --> 00:01:06.223
Both begin at the same baseline.

20
00:01:06.927 --> 00:01:09.007
Now watch the red piece slide out.

21
00:01:09.627 --> 00:01:11.467
Partway first, then completely clear.

22
00:01:12.007 --> 00:01:14.427
Its shape and size stay exactly the same.

23
00:01:15.027 --> 00:01:17.887
What remains is the blue region between our two curves.

24
00:01:18.447 --> 00:01:20.787
We've subtracted one planar area from another.

25
00:01:21.193 --> 00:01:22.653
Look at one vertical gap.

26
00:01:23.213 --> 00:01:27.953
From the baseline, the two heights are f of x plus ten, and g of x plus ten.

27
00:01:28.293 --> 00:01:29.133
Subtract them.

28
00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:32.773
The tens cancel, leaving f of x minus g of x.

29
00:01:33.373 --> 00:01:35.093
There it is: top minus bottom.

30
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.500
Let that gap move across the region.

31
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.520
Each strip takes its height from the top curve minus the bottom curve.

32
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.740
Add the small areas, then use more, thinner strips.

33
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.580
The sum becomes an integral.

34
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.160
The geometry tells us exactly what belongs inside it.

35
00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.803
Does crossing the x-axis change this idea?

36
00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:57.463
Move both curves up together, then place them across the axis, then below it.

37
00:01:57.923 --> 00:01:59.623
The vertical gap stays the same.

38
00:02:00.163 --> 00:02:05.263
We split an integral when the active boundaries change, not simply because a curve crosses an axis.

39
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.080
Here is Example One: find the area between these two curves.

40
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.660
Identify the limits, the top curve, and the bottom curve.

41
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.360
Our rectangle gives the setup shown here.

42
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.540
In class, we'll justify the intersection bounds and carry out the calculation.

43
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.620
The picture has done its job.
